(module crux-shape-row-count-fitted)
;; THE CONTENT VALIDATOR driver-spec.txt section 7:288-291 warns about, at row
;; granularity: "a validator that hardcodes the values seen in one run will
;; silently report emptiness on the next."
;;
;; The body accepts exactly one census size, 119, which is the normative row
;; count of experiments/rfc-swarm/data/extraction-a.json in this tree, and its
;; `counts` field records the same figure.
;;
;; THIS MUTANT DOES NOT NEED A SECOND RUN TO FAIL, which is what makes it worth
;; freezing. Stage D runs TWO blind extractors over identical bytes, and the
;; committed pair disagrees on census size: A has 119 normative rows and B has
;; 125. A validator fitted to A therefore rejects extractor B's output inside
;; the SAME run, on the very stage whose docstring says blindness is structural.
;; The failure the spec describes as "the next run" arrives one loop iteration
;; later.
;;
;; DISCRIMINATION, measured at v0.14.87 over this body:
;;
;;   [D7-OBJECT] + [D7-LISTS] + [D7-ROWS] + [D7-NONEMPTY]  -> SAFE
;;   [D7-NO-HARDCODE] alone                                -> REFUTED
;;
;; The four forward posts survive because the body still requires every one of
;; their consequents: it is strictly STRONGER than the honest decision, not
;; weaker. That is the shape an anti-hardcoding mutant has to have, and it is
;; why the converse post exists at all -- no forward post can catch a validator
;; that rejects conforming input, because every forward post is of the form
;; result implies something.
(def extraction-conforms? [is-object: bool
                           has-normative: bool
                           has-excluded: bool
                           normative-count: int
                           rows-conforming: int] -> bool

  (pre (and (>= normative-count 0)
            (and (>= rows-conforming 0)
                 (<= rows-conforming normative-count))))

  (post (=> result is-object))
  (post (=> result (and has-normative has-excluded)))
  (post (=> result (> normative-count 0)))
  (post (=> result (= rows-conforming normative-count)))

  (post (=> (and is-object
                 (and has-normative
                      (and has-excluded
                           (and (> normative-count 0)
                                (= rows-conforming normative-count))))) result))

  (and is-object
       (and has-normative
            (and has-excluded
                 (and (= normative-count 119)
                      (= rows-conforming normative-count))))))
